  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM EDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nHigh pressure will keep things quiet and dry through the \nweekend and into early next week, with temperatures gradually \nwarming further into the 70s. Tonight will remain cool with \nmostly clear skies and light winds, allowing lows to fall into \nthe upper 30s across interior locations, with most areas in the \n40s to low 50s. Frost potential looks fairly limited, though \nsome of the typical colder interior spots could see localized \nfrost. Sunday then stays dry with mostly sunny skies and highs \nmainly in the upper 60s to low 70s.\n\nThe warming trend continues Monday and Tuesday as the surface \nhigh gradually shifts away, with highs climbing further into the\n70s and some locations potentially reaching the upper 70s by \nTuesday (average highs this time of year are generally 61 to 68 \ndegrees). Though daytime highs will be warmer, the dry airmass \nand clear nights will still allow or fairly cool overnight \ntemperatures. Overall, conditions look quiet and pleasant \nthrough the first half of the week.\n\nThe next chance for precipitation arrives around the middle of \nnext week as a more potent northern-stream wave moves through \nthe Upper Midwest and Great Lakes. This should help pull some of\nthe deeper moisture farther north, but much of the better \nmoisture is still expected to remain focused across the Plains \nand farther south. As a result, shower chances look to increase \nTuesday night and especially Wednesday into Wednesday night, but\nthere is still considerable uncertainty on how much \nprecipitation actually reaches northern Michigan. The timing and\neventual track of the wave will also play a big role in how \nmuch of an impact the system makes on the region and whether we \nsee a more meaningful round of rain or just some scattered \nshowers. For now, continue to keep the forecast on shower \nchances rather than anything more widespread or significant, \nwith the eventual track and moisture availability of the system \nstill being the main questions.",
